The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
clk_unregister(plat_data.hclk);
clk_unregister(plat_data.pclk);
clk_unregister(pclk);
</text>
Reason:
The clocks `hclk` and `pclk` are registered using `clk_register_fixed_rate()`, which allocates a fixed-rate clock with additional metadata. When cleaning up (error paths or removal), the generic `clk_unregister()` is used instead of the specific `clk_unregister_fixed_rate()`. This mismatch means the extra resources allocated by `clk_register_fixed_rate()` (such as the fixed-rate clock structure) are never deallocated, causing a resource leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
clk_unregister_fixed_rate(plat_data.hclk);
clk_unregister_fixed_rate(plat_data.pclk);
clk_unregister_fixed_rate(pclk);
clk_unregister_fixed_rate(hclk);
</text>
Reason:
The fix replaces all `clk_unregister()` calls with `clk_unregister_fixed_rate()`, which is the proper counterpart for clocks obtained via `clk_register_fixed_rate()`. This ensures that the full set of resources allocated during registration (including the fixed-rate-specific data) are correctly freed, preventing memory/resource leaks.

